8. User Flow
Step	Screen	What happens
1	Choose	User picks a form, language, mode (dictation or guided) and voice engine.
2	Speak	User records for about 45 seconds. Live level bars confirm the mic is working. Transcript can be edited.
3	Process	Rules extract structured fields. Cache is checked. If needed, one LLM call fills the rest. Everything is validated locally.
4	Check	Colour-coded form. Missing or low-confidence fields are grouped into short follow-up questions, answered by voice or keyboard (max 2 rounds).
5	Confirm	User edits any field; local validation runs on Confirm (no AI call).
6	PDF	Bilingual, print-ready PDF is downloaded.
9. Non-Functional Requirements
Category	Requirement	Target
Performance	Extraction response (rules + 1 LLM call) on a normal connection	p95 under 6 s
Performance	Cached or rules-only response	under 1 s
Reliability	Graceful degradation when an AI provider fails or is rate-limited	No unhandled errors; rules-only fallback
Cost	LLM calls per completed form	at most 2 (average)
Security	API keys only on server; no keys in frontend or git; friendly errors without stack traces	Required
Privacy	Personal data minimisation; no accounts; no analytics on field values (see Section 11)	Required
Accessibility	Large tap targets, voice-first, bilingual labels, keyboard operable	WCAG 2.1 AA (goal)
Compatibility	Chrome (desktop and Android) primary; HTTPS required for microphone	Required
Scalability	Stateless API; horizontal scale; swap JSON cache for Redis in production	Phase 2
Maintainability	Automated tests; forms defined as data	10 tests passing
10. System Architecture
SpeakFill is a single deployable service: a FastAPI backend that also serves a no-build HTML/CSS/JS frontend. This keeps deployment simple and demos reliable.
Layer	Technology and role
Frontend	Plain HTML, CSS, JavaScript. Web Speech API and MediaRecorder for voice. Poppins and Noto Nastaliq Urdu fonts.
API	FastAPI with Pydantic validation. Endpoints listed in Appendix A.
Pipeline	Rules, then cache, then one LLM call, then local validation, then local question builder.
LLM router	Gemini (primary), Groq Llama (fallback), rules-only (last resort). JSON-only output, temperature 0.
Speech	Browser STT first (free); Groq Whisper fallback and for regional languages.
PDF	ReportLab with arabic-reshaper and python-bidi, Noto Naskh Arabic font.
Hosting	Render (Blueprint), Hugging Face Spaces (Docker) or Railway. HTTPS provided.
Data flow
Voice  >  Speech-to-text  >  Transcript  >  Rule extraction  >  Cache lookup  >  (single) LLM call for the remaining fields  >  Local validation and colour status  >  Local follow-up questions  >  User review  >  PDF.
11. AI Design, Cost Control and Safety
Cost-control techniques
Technique	Effect
One call per form (batched)	25-field form costs 1 call, not 25.
Rules before AI	Regex fills CNIC, phone, email and dates for free; if all fields are filled, 0 LLM calls.
Compact schema	Only id, type and short label are sent, reducing tokens.
Local follow-up questions	Template questions need no AI.
Cache	Repeated transcripts cost nothing.
Rate limiter and session budget	Prevents self-inflicted 429 errors and one user draining the quota.
Single in-flight request	Client and server locks prevent double-spending on double clicks.
Safety and quality
•	The prompt forbids inventing data: unclear fields return null with confidence 0.
•	Spoken numbers are converted to digits; dates are interpreted day-first, as in Pakistani speech.
•	The user always reviews and confirms before PDF creation (human in the loop).
•	Speech recognition errors are tolerated: the model fixes obvious ones and lowers confidence so the field turns yellow.
Privacy and data handling
Forms contain sensitive identifiers (CNIC, phone, address). Requirements:
•	No user accounts and no database of personal data in the MVP.
•	Audio is sent only to the speech provider for transcription; transcripts and values are sent only to the LLM provider chosen by the operator.
•	Production requirement: the on-disk result cache must not persist personal values. Use in-memory only, or store hashed keys with short TTL, and disclose provider processing in a privacy notice.
•	Secrets live in environment variables; .env is git-ignored.
12. Success Metrics
Metric	Definition	Target
Time to complete	Start to PDF download, median	under 3 minutes
Field accuracy	Fields unchanged by the user at Confirm / total fields	at least 90%
Completion rate	Sessions reaching PDF / sessions started	at least 70%
AI efficiency	Average LLM calls per completed form	at most 2
Voice success	Recordings producing usable transcript	at least 90%
User satisfaction	Post-task rating (1 to 5)	at least 4.2
Reliability	Requests ending in a handled outcome (no crash)	at least 99%
13. Competitive Landscape
Alternative	Strength	Gap SpeakFill fills
Typing manually or cyber cafe	Familiar	Slow, costs money, shares private data with a third party.
General chatbots (ChatGPT-style)	Flexible	No form schema, no CNIC/phone validation, no PDF, inconsistent output.
Phone keyboard voice typing	Free, built in	Dictates text into one field at a time; no understanding of the form.
Global form-fill and autofill tools	Polished	Text-first, English-centric, no Urdu/Roman Urdu or Pakistani formats.
14. Business Model and Go-to-Market
Monetisation options
•	B2C freemium: Free for a few forms per month; paid for unlimited forms, saved profile and priority processing.
•	B2B / B2G: Per-seat or per-form pricing for banks, universities, HR agencies and help desks; white-label widget on their sites.
•	API: Voice-to-structured-data for other builders, billed per call.
Go-to-market
•	Pilot with 2 to 3 university admission offices and one bank branch or microfinance partner.
•	Partner with cyber cafes and digital literacy programmes as distribution channels.
•	Short Urdu video demos on social media; WhatsApp sharing of the PDF.
15. Risks and Mitigations
Risk	Level	Mitigation
Free-tier quota or model name changes	High	Provider chain, rate limiter, budget guard, rules-only fallback, configurable model names.
Urdu speech recognition errors	High	Whisper option, editable transcript, confidence colours, review step.
Privacy of sensitive data	High	No accounts, no persistent PII, privacy notice, in-memory cache in production.
Wrong data submitted by trusting AI	Med	Never invent data, mandatory confirmation, local validators.
Free hosting sleeps after inactivity	Med	Warm up before demos; offline demo mode as backup.
Browser limitations (Web Speech API)	Med	Whisper fallback; typed input always available.
Institutions reject printed forms	Med	Pilot partners to approve layouts; template customisation in Phase 2.
16. Hackathon Alignment
How the project maps to typical judging criteria and what to emphasise in the demo.
Criterion	Evidence
Problem and impact	Language and typing barriers in Pakistan; time and money saved; dignity and privacy for first-time applicants.
Innovation	Urdu/Roman Urdu voice to validated form to Urdu PDF; 1 to 2 AI calls per form.
Technical depth	Rules-first hybrid pipeline, provider fallback, rate limiting, caching, bidi PDF rendering, tests.
Usability	Colour-coded review, guided mode, per-field mic, offline demo.
Feasibility and scalability	Forms as JSON; stateless service; clear roadmap; low cost per form.
Business viability	B2C, B2B and API models; clear pilot partners.
3-minute demo script
•	0:00 Problem: long English forms, language barrier (20 s).
•	0:20 Choose Job Application, speak about 45 seconds in Urdu/Roman Urdu.
•	1:10 Show transcript and colour-coded form; point to the header counter (1 AI, 0 voice).
•	1:50 Answer the grouped follow-up questions by voice; edit one field.
•	2:30 Confirm and show the Urdu PDF.
•	2:50 Close with roadmap (OCR upload, partners) and ask. Backup: Offline demo button.
17. Open Questions
•	Which institutions will pilot, and can we obtain their official form templates?
•	Confirm current free-tier limits and model names for Gemini and Groq before launch.
•	Source and cite market-size data (internet users, banked population, applicants per year) for investor material.
•	Legal review of data-protection obligations for CNIC and other identifiers.
Appendix A. API Endpoints
Method	Path	Purpose
GET	/api/health	Active providers, STT fallback, Urdu font, form count
GET	/api/forms	List built-in forms
GET	/api/forms/{id}	Full schema of one form
GET	/api/usage	Per-session AI, voice and cached counters
GET	/api/demo	Offline demo transcript and answers
POST	/api/transcribe	Audio to text (Whisper fallback)
POST	/api/extract	Transcript to filled fields with confidence and questions
POST	/api/clarify	Resolve unresolved fields (0 or 1 LLM call)
POST	/api/validate	Local validation only (guided mode, Confirm)
POST	/api/pdf	Validated values to PDF
Appendix B. Built-in Forms and Field Types
Form	Fields	Notes
Job Application	13	Name, father's name, CNIC, DOB, phone, email, city, address, etc.
Bank Account Opening	13	Identity and contact details with select options
University Admission	13	Personal and academic details
Complaint Letter	8	Includes free-text complaint (textarea) kept in speaker's language
Supported field types: text, cnic, phone, date, email, select, number, address, textarea. Each form is a JSON file with bilingual labels, required flag, example and optional validation rules.
